INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00288', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 0, 'When was the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) established, and what is notable about its ownership history?', 'The **Reserve Bank of India** was established on **1 April 1935** under the **Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934**, following the recommendations of the Hilton Young Commission.

It was originally set up as a **privately owned institution**, and was **nationalised in 1949**, after which it has functioned as a wholly government-owned central bank, headquartered in **Mumbai**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00289', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 1, 'When was SEBI established, and when did it acquire statutory powers?', 'The **Securities and Exchange Board of India (SEBI)** was initially set up in **1988** as a **non-statutory body** to regulate the securities market.

It acquired **statutory powers in 1992** through the **SEBI Act, 1992**, which simultaneously repealed the Capital Issues (Control) Act, 1947. SEBI is headquartered in the **Bandra Kurla Complex, Mumbai**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00290', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 2, 'What does IRDAI stand for, and what does it regulate?', '**IRDAI** stands for the **Insurance Regulatory and Development Authority of India**.

Established in **1999** under the IRDA Act, it regulates and promotes the **insurance and reinsurance industry** in India, licensing insurers and protecting policyholders'' interests. It is headquartered in **Hyderabad**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00291', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 3, 'What does NABARD stand for, and what is its primary role?', '**NABARD** stands for the **National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development**.

Established on **12 July 1982**, it functions as India''s **apex development bank** for agriculture and rural development, providing refinance support to banks and cooperative institutions for rural credit. It is headquartered in **Mumbai**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00292', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 4, 'What does PFRDA stand for, and what does it regulate?', '**PFRDA** stands for the **Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority**.

Established in **2003**, it regulates and promotes the **pension sector** in India, including the **National Pension System (NPS)**, to ensure the orderly growth of the pension industry and protect subscribers'' interests.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00293', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 5, 'What is the role of the Competition Commission of India (CCI)?', 'The **Competition Commission of India (CCI)** is the statutory body responsible for **enforcing competition law** in India, established under the **Competition Act, 2002**.

Its core mandate is to **prevent practices that harm competition**, such as cartels and abuse of dominant market position, and to **regulate mergers and acquisitions** that could adversely affect competition in Indian markets.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00294', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 6, 'What is a Credit Rating Agency, and name two prominent ones operating in India.', 'A **Credit Rating Agency** is an organisation that assesses the **creditworthiness** of a borrower — a company, financial instrument, or even a government — and assigns a rating indicating the likelihood of timely repayment of debt.

Two prominent credit rating agencies operating in India are **CRISIL** and **ICRA** (a third major one is **CARE Ratings**), each of which evaluates corporate bonds, bank loans, and other debt instruments.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00295', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 7, 'When was the United Nations (UN) founded, where is it headquartered, and how many permanent members does its Security Council have?', 'The **United Nations** was founded on **24 October 1945**, after the end of World War II, and is headquartered in **New York City**.

Its **Security Council** has **5 permanent members** — the **United States, United Kingdom, France, Russia, and China** — each holding veto power, along with 10 non-permanent members elected for two-year terms.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00296', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 8, 'When was the International Monetary Fund (IMF) established, and where is it headquartered?', 'The **International Monetary Fund (IMF)** was established in **1944** at the **Bretton Woods Conference**, alongside the World Bank, and began operations in **1945**.

It is headquartered in **Washington, D.C.**, and its primary role is to promote **global monetary cooperation and financial stability**, including providing short-term balance-of-payments support to member countries.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00297', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 9, 'When was the World Bank established, and what distinguishes its core role from the IMF''s?', 'The **World Bank** was also established in **1944** at the **Bretton Woods Conference**, and is headquartered in **Washington, D.C.**, alongside the IMF.

While the IMF focuses on **short-term financial stability and balance-of-payments support**, the World Bank''s core role is to provide **long-term development financing** — loans and grants for infrastructure, poverty reduction, and structural development projects in member countries.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00298', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 10, 'When was the World Trade Organization (WTO) established, where is it headquartered, and what preceded it?', 'The **World Trade Organization (WTO)** was established on **1 January 1995**, and is headquartered in **Geneva, Switzerland**.

It succeeded the **General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT)**, which had governed international trade rules since **1947**; the WTO''s core function is to administer global trade agreements and resolve trade disputes between member nations.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00299', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 11, 'When was the World Health Organization (WHO) founded, and where is it headquartered?', 'The **World Health Organization (WHO)** was founded in **1948** as a **specialized agency of the United Nations**.

It is headquartered in **Geneva, Switzerland**, and its mandate is to direct and coordinate international public health efforts, including disease surveillance, health emergency response, and setting global health standards.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00300', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 12, 'When was the International Labour Organization (ILO) founded, and what is distinctive about its governance structure?', 'The **International Labour Organization (ILO)** was founded in **1919**, making it one of the **oldest specialized agencies** associated with international governance (it predates the UN itself, later becoming its first specialized agency in 1946).

It is headquartered in **Geneva, Switzerland**, and is distinctive for its **tripartite structure**, in which representatives of governments, employers, and workers all participate in its decision-making.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00301', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 13, 'What is the key difference in the core mandates of the IMF and the World Bank?', 'The **IMF** primarily addresses **short-term macroeconomic and balance-of-payments stability**, lending to countries facing currency or external payment crises, and monitoring global financial and exchange-rate systems.

The **World Bank**, by contrast, focuses on **long-term economic development**, financing infrastructure, education, and poverty-reduction projects in developing countries — the two institutions are complementary rather than competing.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00302', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 14, 'What is GDP, and how does it differ from GNP?', '**GDP (Gross Domestic Product)** measures the total monetary value of all goods and services produced **within a country''s geographic borders** in a given period, regardless of who owns the producing entity (domestic or foreign).

**GNP (Gross National Product)** measures the total output produced by a **country''s residents and companies**, whether located domestically or abroad, but excludes output produced within the country by foreign-owned entities.

The relationship is `GNP = GDP + Net Income from Abroad`.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00303', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 15, 'What is Fiscal Deficit, and how is it typically expressed?', '**Fiscal Deficit** is the difference between a government''s **total expenditure** and its **total revenue (excluding borrowings)** in a given financial year — it represents the amount the government must borrow to meet its spending commitments.

It is typically expressed as a **percentage of GDP**, which allows comparison of deficit levels across years and countries of different economic sizes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00304', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 16, 'What is NITI Aayog, and when did it replace the Planning Commission?', '**NITI Aayog** (National Institution for Transforming India) is the **policy think tank** of the Government of India, established in **2015** to replace the **Planning Commission**, which had existed since 1950.

Unlike the Planning Commission, which allocated central funds to states through Five-Year Plans, NITI Aayog functions primarily as an **advisory and policy-formulation body**, without the power to allocate plan funds.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00305', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 17, 'How many Five-Year Plans did India implement before the system was discontinued, and what replaced it?', 'India implemented **12 Five-Year Plans** in total, starting with the **First Five-Year Plan (1951-56)** and ending with the **Twelfth Five-Year Plan (2012-17)**.

The system was **discontinued after 2017**, replaced by **NITI Aayog''s** longer-term strategy documents, including 15-year vision documents, 7-year strategy papers, and 3-year action agendas.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00306', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 18, 'What are the main components of India''s Goods and Services Tax (GST) structure?', 'India''s GST operates under a **dual structure**, comprising **CGST (Central GST)** and **SGST (State GST)**, both levied simultaneously on intra-state transactions.

For inter-state transactions, **IGST (Integrated GST)** is levied by the Centre; **UTGST (Union Territory GST)** applies in place of SGST for transactions within Union Territories without their own legislature.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00307', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 19, 'When was GST implemented in India?', 'The **Goods and Services Tax (GST)** was implemented in India with effect from **1 July 2017**, following the passage of the **101st Constitutional Amendment Act**.

It replaced a complex web of indirect taxes (including excise duty, service tax, and VAT) with a **single, unified indirect tax** structure across the country, and 1 July is now observed annually as **GST Day**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00308', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 20, 'When did India carry out its major demonetisation of high-value currency notes, and which notes were affected?', 'India''s major demonetisation took place on **8 November 2016**, when the Prime Minister announced that the **Rs. 500 and Rs. 1000 currency notes** would cease to be legal tender, effective from midnight.

The stated objectives included curbing **black money, counterfeit currency, and corruption**, and promoting a shift toward digital transactions.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00309', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 21, 'When did India nationalise its major commercial banks, and how many banks were nationalised in each phase?', 'India nationalised **14 major commercial banks** in **1969**, each having deposits above a specified threshold, under Prime Minister Indira Gandhi''s government.

A second phase of nationalisation occurred in **1980**, bringing **6 more banks** under government ownership, with the stated aim of extending banking access and credit to rural and underserved sectors of the economy.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00310', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 22, 'What is meant by ''Disinvestment'' in the context of government economic policy?', '**Disinvestment** refers to the process by which the government **sells or liquidates its ownership stake** (fully or partially) in public sector undertakings (PSUs) or other assets.

It is typically undertaken to **raise revenue, reduce fiscal burden, improve operational efficiency**, or reduce the government''s role in sectors better served by private enterprise, and can range from a small equity sale to complete privatisation.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00311', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 23, 'When was the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) established, and what distinguishes its historical significance?', 'The **Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE)** was established on **9 July 1875**, originally as the ''Native Share & Stock Brokers'' Association''.

It is recognised as **Asia''s oldest stock exchange**, and is located on **Dalal Street, Mumbai**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00312', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 24, 'When was the National Stock Exchange (NSE) incorporated, and when did it begin trading operations?', 'The **National Stock Exchange (NSE)** was incorporated in **1992** and received recognition as a stock exchange from SEBI in **April 1993**.

It officially **began trading operations in 1994**, and introduced electronic, screen-based trading to the Indian market, in contrast to the open-outcry system used earlier.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00313', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 25, 'How many companies comprise the BSE Sensex, and what is its base year and base value?', 'The **BSE Sensex** comprises **30** of the largest and most actively traded companies listed on the Bombay Stock Exchange.

Its **base year is 1978-79**, with a **base value of 100** — meaning the index''s current value reflects growth in free-float market capitalisation relative to that base period.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00314', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 26, 'How many companies comprise the Nifty 50 index, and what is its base date and base value?', 'The **Nifty 50**, India''s flagship index on the National Stock Exchange, comprises **50** large-cap companies across various sectors.

Its **base date is 3 November 1995**, with a **base value of 1,000**, and the index was officially launched in **April 1996**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00315', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 27, 'What is a ''Circuit Breaker'' in the context of stock markets?', 'A **Circuit Breaker** is a regulatory mechanism that **automatically halts trading** on a stock exchange (or in a specific stock) when prices move beyond a pre-defined percentage limit within a short period, either upward or downward.

Its purpose is to **curb excessive volatility and panic-driven trading**, giving market participants time to absorb information and make more rational decisions before trading resumes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00316', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 28, 'What is the difference between an IPO and an FPO?', 'An **IPO (Initial Public Offering)** is the **first time** a private company offers its shares to the public, thereby becoming a publicly listed company.

An **FPO (Follow-on Public Offering)** refers to any **subsequent issuance of shares** to the public by a company that is **already listed** on a stock exchange, typically undertaken to raise additional capital.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00317', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 29, 'What does CAGR stand for, and what is its formula?', '**CAGR** stands for **Compound Annual Growth Rate** — the mean annual growth rate of an investment or metric over a specified period longer than one year, assuming growth is compounded annually.

Its formula is `CAGR = (Ending Value / Beginning Value)^(1/n) - 1`, where `n` is the number of years.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00318', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 30, 'What does EPS stand for, and what does it measure?', '**EPS** stands for **Earnings Per Share** — a widely used measure of a company''s profitability on a per-share basis.

It is calculated as `EPS = Net Profit / Number of Outstanding Shares`, and is a key input used by investors to assess how much profit is attributable to each share they hold.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00319', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 31, 'What does the P/E ratio measure, and how is it calculated?', 'The **P/E (Price-to-Earnings) ratio** measures how much investors are willing to pay for each rupee (or dollar) of a company''s earnings, serving as a common valuation metric.

It is calculated as `P/E = Market Price per Share / Earnings per Share (EPS)` — a higher P/E generally suggests investors expect higher future growth, while a lower P/E can suggest undervaluation or weaker growth expectations.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00320', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 32, 'What does NPA stand for in banking, and what does it signify?', '**NPA** stands for **Non-Performing Asset**.

It refers to a loan or advance for which the **principal or interest payment has remained overdue for a period of 90 days or more** — such loans are considered to have stopped generating income for the lending bank and signal credit risk on its balance sheet.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00321', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 33, 'What is the key difference between FDI and FII?', '**FDI (Foreign Direct Investment)** refers to investment made by a foreign entity to acquire a **lasting management interest or controlling stake** in a domestic business — typically involving setting up operations or acquiring substantial ownership.

**FII (Foreign Institutional Investment)** refers to investment by foreign institutions in **financial assets** such as stocks and bonds, without seeking control or management influence — FII flows are generally considered more volatile (''hot money'') than FDI.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00322', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 34, 'What does CSR stand for, and what is the mandated spending requirement under India''s Companies Act, 2013?', '**CSR** stands for **Corporate Social Responsibility**.

Under the **Companies Act, 2013**, companies meeting specified thresholds of net worth, turnover, or net profit are required to spend at least **2% of their average net profit** (of the preceding three financial years) on CSR activities — making India one of the first countries to **mandate** CSR spending by law.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00323', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 35, 'What does ESG stand for in the context of corporate and investment evaluation?', '**ESG** stands for **Environmental, Social, and Governance** — a framework used to evaluate a company''s practices and performance beyond pure financial metrics.

**Environmental** covers factors like carbon footprint and resource use; **Social** covers labour practices and community impact; **Governance** covers board structure, executive pay, and shareholder rights — ESG scores are increasingly used by investors to assess sustainability and ethical impact.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00324', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 36, 'What is a ''Unicorn'' in the startup ecosystem?', 'A **Unicorn** is a privately held startup company that has reached a **valuation of US$1 billion or more**, without yet being listed on a public stock exchange.

The term was coined in 2013 by venture capitalist Aileen Lee, reflecting how rare such high-valuation startups were considered at the time.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00325', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 37, 'What is CPI, and what is its current base year in India?', '**CPI (Consumer Price Index)** measures the average change over time in the prices paid by **consumers** for a representative basket of goods and services, and is the primary measure used to track **retail inflation**.

In India, the all-India CPI currently uses **2012 as its base year** (2012 = 100).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00326', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 38, 'What is WPI, and what is its current base year in India?', '**WPI (Wholesale Price Index)** measures the average change in prices of goods at the **wholesale (first bulk sale) stage**, before they reach retail consumers, and primarily reflects price trends from the perspective of producers and traders.

In India, the WPI currently uses **2011-12 as its base year** (2011-12 = 100), having been revised from an earlier base year of 2004-05.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00327', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 39, 'What is the Human Development Index (HDI), and what three dimensions does it combine?', 'The **Human Development Index (HDI)** is a composite statistic, first published by the **UNDP in 1990** (developed under the leadership of economist **Mahbub ul Haq**, with conceptual input from **Amartya Sen**), designed to measure development beyond income alone.

It combines three dimensions: **life expectancy at birth** (health), **mean and expected years of schooling** (education), and **Gross National Income per capita** (standard of living).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00328', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 40, 'What is the Gini coefficient, and what range of values does it take?', 'The **Gini coefficient** is a statistical measure of **income or wealth inequality** within a population, originally developed by Italian statistician **Corrado Gini in 1912**, building on the concept of the Lorenz curve.

It ranges from **0 to 1**, where **0 represents perfect equality** (everyone has identical income) and **1 represents perfect inequality** (one person holds all the income).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00329', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 41, 'What is Purchasing Power Parity (PPP), and why is it used in economic comparisons?', '**Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)** is an economic concept that compares different countries'' currencies by evaluating the **cost of an identical basket of goods and services** in each country, rather than relying solely on market exchange rates.

It is used because market exchange rates can distort cross-country comparisons of living standards or economic output — PPP-adjusted figures are commonly used to compare **real GDP** across countries with very different price levels.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00330', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 42, 'What is the Repo Rate, and how does it function as a monetary policy tool?', 'The **Repo Rate** is the rate at which a country''s central bank (such as the RBI) lends **short-term funds to commercial banks**, against government securities as collateral, under a repurchase agreement.

When the central bank **raises** the repo rate, borrowing becomes costlier for banks, which typically pass this on to customers, cooling down credit growth and inflation; **lowering** it has the opposite, stimulative effect.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00331', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 43, 'What is the Reverse Repo Rate?', 'The **Reverse Repo Rate** is the rate at which a country''s central bank **borrows funds from commercial banks**, effectively the mirror image of the repo rate.

It is used to **absorb excess liquidity** from the banking system — raising the reverse repo rate encourages banks to park more surplus funds with the central bank rather than lend them out, helping to control inflation during periods of excess liquidity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00332', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 44, 'What is the Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)?', 'The **Cash Reserve Ratio (CRR)** is the minimum percentage of a bank''s **total deposits** that it must maintain as cash reserves with the central bank, and cannot lend out or invest.

Adjusting the CRR is a tool of monetary policy: **raising** the CRR reduces the funds banks have available to lend (tightening liquidity), while **lowering** it increases lendable funds (easing liquidity).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00333', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 45, 'What is the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)?', 'The **Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR)** is the minimum percentage of a bank''s **net demand and time liabilities (deposits)** that it must maintain in the form of liquid assets, such as cash, gold, or approved government securities, before extending credit to customers.

Unlike CRR (held with the central bank), SLR reserves are held **by the bank itself**, and the ratio is used to regulate bank credit growth and ensure the solvency of banks.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00334', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 46, 'What is the difference between the Bank Rate and the Repo Rate?', 'The **Bank Rate** is the rate at which the central bank lends funds to commercial banks **without any collateral**, typically for longer-term borrowing needs.

The **Repo Rate** is the rate for **short-term, collateralised borrowing** (against government securities) under a repurchase agreement. In practice, repo rate changes are used far more actively as the primary tool of monetary policy, while the bank rate is used less frequently and is usually kept aligned with the Marginal Standing Facility rate.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00335', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 47, 'What is the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), and what is its primary responsibility?', 'The **Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)** is a statutory, six-member committee, established in **2016**, responsible for determining India''s **key policy interest rates** (notably the repo rate) to achieve the inflation target set by the Government of India in consultation with the RBI.

Its primary responsibility is to achieve **price stability** while keeping growth considerations in mind, and its decisions are made by **majority vote** among its members, which include RBI officials and external experts.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00336', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 48, 'When was the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences first awarded, and to whom?', 'The **Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences** (formally the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel) was first awarded in **1969**.

The inaugural prize was shared by **Ragnar Frisch** (Norway) and **Jan Tinbergen** (Netherlands), honoured for developing and applying **dynamic models for the analysis of economic processes**. Unlike the original Nobel Prizes, it was established by Sweden''s central bank in 1968, not by Alfred Nobel''s will.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00337', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 49, 'Who was the first Asian economist to win the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences, and for what contribution?', '**Amartya Sen**, an Indian economist, won the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in **1998**, becoming the **first Asian** to receive this honour.

He was recognised for his contributions to **welfare economics**, including his work on social choice theory, poverty measurement, and famine analysis, which reshaped how economists think about well-being and development.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00338', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 50, 'Which economist is most closely associated with ''Monetarism'', and when did he win the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences?', '**Milton Friedman** is most closely associated with **Monetarism**, the school of thought emphasising that controlling the **money supply** is the primary tool for managing inflation and stabilising the economy.

He won the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in **1976**, cited for his work on consumption analysis, monetary history and theory, and the complexity of stabilisation policy.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00339', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 51, 'For what contribution did Abhijit Banerjee, Esther Duflo, and Michael Kremer jointly win the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences in 2019?', '**Abhijit Banerjee, Esther Duflo, and Michael Kremer** won the 2019 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences for their **experimental approach to alleviating global poverty**.

They pioneered the use of **randomized controlled trials (RCTs)** — a method borrowed from medical research — to rigorously test which development interventions (in areas like education and healthcare) actually work on the ground, transforming development economics into a more evidence-based field.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00340', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 52, 'Muhammad Yunus is widely associated with microfinance — which Nobel Prize did he win, and for what institution is he known?', '**Muhammad Yunus** won the **Nobel Peace Prize in 2006** (not the Economics prize), shared jointly with the institution he founded, the **Grameen Bank**.

He is recognised as a pioneer of **microfinance and microcredit**, providing small, collateral-free loans to poor entrepreneurs — particularly women — in Bangladesh, aimed at fostering economic and social development from the grassroots.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00341', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 53, 'Who is widely regarded as the ''Father of Economics'', and what is his most famous work?', '**Adam Smith**, the 18th-century Scottish economist and philosopher, is widely regarded as the **''Father of Economics''** (or the father of modern economics).

His most famous work, **''An Inquiry into the Nature and Causes of the Wealth of Nations''** (1776), laid foundational concepts for classical economics, including the idea of the **''invisible hand''** guiding free markets toward efficient outcomes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00342', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 54, 'What is the ''Make in India'' initiative, and when was it launched?', '**''Make in India''** is a flagship initiative launched by the **Government of India in September 2014**, aimed at encouraging both domestic and foreign companies to **manufacture their products within India**.

Its broader goals include boosting manufacturing''s share of GDP, generating employment, and attracting **foreign direct investment (FDI)** across targeted sectors.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00343', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 55, 'When was the current Companies Act, which governs corporate regulation in India, enacted?', 'The **Companies Act, 2013** was enacted to replace the earlier Companies Act of 1956, and governs the incorporation, regulation, and dissolution of companies in India.

Among its notable provisions is the **mandatory CSR (Corporate Social Responsibility) spending requirement**, making India one of the first countries globally to legally mandate such spending for qualifying companies.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00344', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 56, 'What is the fundamental difference between a direct tax and an indirect tax?', 'A **direct tax** is levied directly on an individual''s or entity''s **income or wealth**, and its burden **cannot be shifted** to someone else — examples include income tax and corporate tax.

An **indirect tax** is levied on the **sale of goods or services**, and its burden **can be passed on** from the seller to the end consumer through the price of the product — examples include GST and customs duty.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00345', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 57, 'What is meant by a ''Blue Chip'' company?', 'A **''Blue Chip'' company** refers to a large, well-established, and financially sound corporation with a **long track record of stable earnings, reliable dividends, and strong market reputation**.

The term originates from poker, where the **blue-coloured chips** traditionally carry the highest value — blue chip stocks are generally considered lower-risk, stable, long-term investments relative to smaller or newer companies.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00346', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 58, 'What is the difference between ''Disinvestment'' and ''Privatization''?', '**Disinvestment** refers broadly to the government **reducing or selling off its stake** in a public sector undertaking — this can be a **partial** sale, with the government retaining majority ownership and control.

**Privatization** specifically refers to the transfer of **majority ownership and management control** of a public enterprise to private hands — so while all privatization involves disinvestment, not all disinvestment amounts to privatization.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00347', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 59, 'What is the key distinction between a ''Merger'' and an ''Acquisition''?', 'A **Merger** occurs when two companies, typically of **comparable size**, combine to form a **new single entity**, often by mutual agreement, with shareholders of both original companies holding stakes in the combined firm.

An **Acquisition** occurs when one company **purchases and absorbs another**, with the acquired company often ceasing to exist as an independent entity — acquisitions can be friendly or hostile, and typically involve a clear ''buyer'' and ''target''.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00348', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 60, 'When was SAARC founded, and how many member countries does it currently have?', 'The **South Asian Association for Regional Cooperation (SAARC)** was founded on **8 December 1985** in Dhaka, with its secretariat based in **Kathmandu, Nepal**.

It originally had **7 founding members** (Bangladesh, Bhutan, India, Maldives, Nepal, Pakistan, and Sri Lanka); **Afghanistan** later joined, bringing the current total to **8 member countries**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00349', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 61, 'Where did the term ''BRIC'' (later ''BRICS'') originate, and when did the group hold its first formal summit?', 'The term **''BRIC''** (Brazil, Russia, India, China) was coined in **2001** by **Jim O''Neill**, an economist at Goldman Sachs, in a paper highlighting these economies'' growing global significance.

The group held its **first formal leaders'' summit in 2009**; **South Africa** joined in 2010, after which the grouping became known as **''BRICS''**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00350', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 62, 'Why did the World Bank discontinue its ''Ease of Doing Business'' report, and when?', 'The **World Bank discontinued its ''Doing Business'' report in September 2021**, after an internal review found **data irregularities** affecting the rankings of certain countries in the 2018 and 2020 editions.

An investigation found that changes to specific countries'' data appeared to have been influenced by senior staff, undermining the report''s credibility as an independent measure of business-climate rankings.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00351', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 63, 'What is the Balance of Payments (BoP), and what are its two main components?', 'The **Balance of Payments (BoP)** is a systematic record of **all economic transactions** between a country''s residents and the rest of the world over a given period.

Its two main components are the **Current Account** (covering trade in goods and services, income, and transfers) and the **Capital Account** (covering cross-border investment and asset flows) — together, these must balance in the overall BoP statement.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00352', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 64, 'What is a Current Account Deficit (CAD)?', 'A **Current Account Deficit (CAD)** occurs when a country''s **total imports of goods, services, and transfers exceed its total exports** of the same, over a given period — meaning the country is a net borrower from the rest of the world on its current account.

It is typically expressed as a **percentage of GDP**, and a persistently high CAD can signal overreliance on foreign capital inflows to finance domestic consumption or investment.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00353', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 65, 'What are the main components of a country''s Foreign Exchange Reserves?', 'A country''s **Foreign Exchange Reserves** typically comprise four components: **Foreign Currency Assets** (holdings in major reserve currencies like the US dollar), **Gold Reserves**, the **Special Drawing Rights (SDRs)** allocated by the IMF, and the **Reserve Tranche Position** (a country''s own reserve position with the IMF).

Of these, Foreign Currency Assets typically form the **largest share** of most countries'' total reserves.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00354', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 66, 'What are Special Drawing Rights (SDRs), and when were they created?', '**Special Drawing Rights (SDRs)** are an international reserve asset created by the **IMF in 1969** to supplement member countries'' existing official reserves.

Importantly, an SDR is **not a currency** itself, but rather a potential claim on the freely usable currencies of IMF members; its value is determined by a **basket of major currencies** (currently the US dollar, euro, Chinese renminbi, Japanese yen, and British pound).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00355', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 67, 'What was the Bretton Woods Conference, and what two major institutions did it establish?', 'The **Bretton Woods Conference** was held in **1944** in Bretton Woods, New Hampshire (USA), bringing together representatives of 44 Allied nations to design a new international monetary and financial order for the post-World War II era.

It established two major institutions that remain central to global finance today: the **International Monetary Fund (IMF)** and the **International Bank for Reconstruction and Development**, which evolved into the **World Bank**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00356', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 68, 'What is a PSU, and what does India''s Maharatna-Navratna-Miniratna classification scheme represent?', 'A **PSU (Public Sector Undertaking)** is a company in which the government (central or state) holds a majority equity stake (typically 51% or more).

India classifies its central PSUs into a tiered system — **Maharatna, Navratna, and Miniratna** — based on criteria like financial performance, turnover, and net worth; this classification grants qualifying PSUs greater **financial and operational autonomy**, such as higher investment limits without requiring prior government approval.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00357', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 69, 'What does the name ''SENSEX'' stand for?', '**SENSEX** is a portmanteau of the words **''Sensitive''** and **''Index''**.

The name was coined for the BSE''s benchmark index to reflect its role in capturing the overall **''sentiment'' or price sensitivity** of the broader Indian stock market through the performance of 30 of its most actively traded, large-cap stocks.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00358', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'General Awareness & Business GK', 4, 70, 'What is a Non-Banking Financial Company (NBFC), and how does it differ from a traditional bank?', 'A **Non-Banking Financial Company (NBFC)** is a company registered under the Companies Act that provides bank-like financial services — such as loans, asset financing, and investment products — but **does not hold a banking license**.

The key difference is that an NBFC **cannot accept demand deposits** (like savings or current account deposits) and is **not part of the payment and settlement system**, meaning it cannot issue cheques drawn on itself the way a bank can; NBFCs are regulated by the RBI but under a distinct regulatory framework from banks.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00359', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 0, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Ubiquitous''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''omnipresent''** or **''widespread''**.

''Ubiquitous'' describes something that appears to be present **everywhere at once** — for instance, ''smartphones have become ubiquitous in modern life.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00360', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 1, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Pragmatic''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''practical''** or **''realistic''**.

''Pragmatic'' describes an approach that deals with problems in a **sensible, results-oriented way**, rather than following rigid theory or idealism.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00361', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 2, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Ephemeral''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''transient''** or **''fleeting''**.

''Ephemeral'' describes something that **lasts for only a very short time**, such as a trend or a fashion that quickly fades.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00362', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 3, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Austere''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''stern''** or, in reference to style, **''unadorned/simple''**.

''Austere'' can describe either a **severe, strict manner** (an austere teacher) or a **plain, minimalist style** lacking any luxury or decoration (austere living conditions).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00363', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 4, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Candid''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''frank''** or **''forthright''**.

''Candid'' describes speech or behaviour that is **truthful and direct**, even when the truth may be uncomfortable to hear.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00364', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 5, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Pernicious''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''harmful''** or **''destructive''**.

''Pernicious'' typically describes harm that is **gradual, subtle, and insidious** rather than immediately obvious — for example, ''the pernicious effects of misinformation.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00365', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 6, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Meticulous''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''thorough''** or **''precise''**.

''Meticulous'' describes someone who shows **great care and attention to detail**, often to the point of being painstaking in their work.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00366', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 7, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Volatile''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''unstable''** or **''unpredictable''**.

''Volatile'' is commonly used to describe markets or situations that are **prone to sudden, sharp changes**, such as ''volatile stock prices.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00367', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 8, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Lucrative''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''profitable''**.

''Lucrative'' describes a venture, deal, or career that **generates substantial financial gain**, such as ''a lucrative business opportunity.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00368', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 9, 'What is a suitable synonym for ''Tenacious''?', 'A suitable synonym is **''persistent''** or **''determined''**.

''Tenacious'' describes someone who **holds firmly to a course of action or goal**, refusing to give up despite obstacles.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00369', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 10, 'What is the antonym of ''Frugal''?', 'The antonym is **''extravagant''** (or ''wasteful'').

While ''frugal'' describes careful, economical spending, **''extravagant''** describes spending that is excessive and lacking restraint.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00370', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 11, 'What is the antonym of ''Benevolent''?', 'The antonym is **''malevolent''** (or ''malicious'').

While ''benevolent'' describes a kind, well-meaning disposition, **''malevolent''** describes an intent to cause harm or ill will toward others.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00371', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 12, 'What is the antonym of ''Transparent'' (as used in the sense of openness, e.g., in governance or business)?', 'The antonym is **''opaque''**.

While a ''transparent'' process is open and easy to see through and understand, an **''opaque''** process is concealed or deliberately difficult to scrutinise.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00372', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 13, 'What is the antonym of ''Abundant''?', 'The antonym is **''scarce''**.

While ''abundant'' describes something available in **large quantities**, **''scarce''** describes something in **short, insufficient supply**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00373', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 14, 'What is the antonym of ''Verbose''?', 'The antonym is **''concise''** (or ''terse'').

While ''verbose'' describes writing or speech that uses **far more words than necessary**, **''concise''** describes communication that is **brief and to the point**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00374', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 15, 'What is the antonym of ''Optimistic''?', 'The antonym is **''pessimistic''**.

While an ''optimistic'' outlook expects **favourable outcomes**, a **''pessimistic''** outlook expects the **worst possible outcome**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00375', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 16, 'What is the antonym of ''Ambiguous''?', 'The antonym is **''unambiguous''** (or ''clear/unequivocal'').

While an ''ambiguous'' statement is **open to multiple interpretations**, an **''unambiguous''** statement has a **single, clear meaning**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00376', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 17, 'What is the antonym of ''Voluntary''?', 'The antonym is **''mandatory''** (or ''compulsory'').

While a ''voluntary'' action is undertaken **by free choice**, a **''mandatory''** action is **required by rule or law**, leaving no choice in the matter.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00377', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 18, 'What is the antonym of ''Surplus''?', 'The antonym is **''deficit''** (or ''shortage'').

While a ''surplus'' refers to an amount **left over after requirements are met**, a **''deficit''** refers to a **shortfall below what is required**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00378', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 19, 'What is the antonym of ''Credible''?', 'The antonym is **''dubious''** (or ''incredible/implausible'').

While a ''credible'' claim is **believable and trustworthy**, a **''dubious''** claim is one that invites **doubt or suspicion**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00379', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 20, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A person who spends money in an extravagant and wasteful manner.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Spendthrift''**.

This term specifically describes someone who habitually spends money **recklessly, without regard to its proper or prudent use**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00380', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 21, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A legal document stating how a person''s property should be distributed after their death.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Will''** (also known as a **''Testament''**).

Such a document legally records the deceased person''s wishes regarding the **distribution of their assets and estate**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00381', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 22, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A person who has extensive knowledge and skill across many different subjects or fields.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Polymath''**.

This term describes someone whose expertise **spans multiple disciplines** — a well-known historical example is Leonardo da Vinci, who excelled in art, science, and engineering alike.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00382', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 23, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A person who seeks to promote the welfare of others, especially through generous donation of money.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Philanthropist''**.

This term describes an individual who actively works to **improve human welfare**, typically through charitable giving or social initiatives.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00383', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 24, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A statement or claim that cannot be proved wrong or disputed.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Irrefutable''**.

This adjective describes evidence or an argument so strong that it is **impossible to deny or disprove**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00384', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 25, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A person who believes that all people are equal and deserve equal rights.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Egalitarian''**.

This term describes someone who upholds the principle of **equality for all individuals**, regardless of background or status.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00385', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 26, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A place where a country''s currency (coins and notes) is officially manufactured.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Mint''**.

A mint is the facility, typically government-controlled, responsible for **producing a nation''s official currency**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00386', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 27, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A form of government in which power rests with a small group of people.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Oligarchy''**.

In this system of governance, **a select few individuals or families hold concentrated power**, rather than power being broadly distributed among citizens or elected representatives.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00387', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 28, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''The scientific study of population statistics, such as births, deaths, and disease.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Demography''**.

This field analyses **population size, structure, and change over time**, and is widely used in economic planning and public policy.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00388', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 29, 'Give the one-word substitution for: ''A person who is new to a field, profession, or activity; a beginner.''', 'The one-word substitution is **''Neophyte''** (or ''novice'').

This term describes someone who has **recently started** learning or practising something, and therefore lacks extensive experience in it.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00389', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 30, 'What does the idiom ''bite the bullet'' mean?', 'It means to **face a difficult or unpleasant situation with courage**, accepting it as necessary even though it is painful.

For example: ''The company had to bite the bullet and lay off staff to survive the downturn.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00390', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 31, 'What does the idiom ''cut corners'' mean?', 'It means to **do something in the easiest, cheapest, or quickest way**, often at the expense of quality or proper procedure.

For example: ''The contractor cut corners on safety checks to finish the project faster.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00391', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 32, 'What does the idiom ''back to square one'' mean?', 'It means to **return to the very beginning** after an attempt has failed, with no progress retained.

For example: ''When the merger talks collapsed, both companies were back to square one.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00392', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 33, 'What does the idiom ''read between the lines'' mean?', 'It means to **understand the implied or hidden meaning** of something, beyond what is explicitly stated.

For example: ''Reading between the lines of the CEO''s statement, it was clear layoffs were coming.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00393', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 34, 'What does the idiom ''a blessing in disguise'' mean?', 'It means something that initially **appears to be bad or unfortunate but ultimately turns out to be beneficial**.

For example: ''Losing that client was a blessing in disguise — it freed up time for a much bigger contract.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00394', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 35, 'What does the idiom ''call it a day'' mean?', 'It means to **decide to stop doing something**, typically work, for the time being.

For example: ''After twelve hours at the office, the team decided to call it a day.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00395', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 36, 'What does the idiom ''cut to the chase'' mean?', 'It means to **get straight to the main point**, without wasting time on unnecessary detail.

For example: ''Let''s cut to the chase — can we meet the deadline or not?''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00396', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 37, 'What does the phrase ''in the pipeline'' mean?', 'It means something is **currently being planned, developed, or processed**, and is expected to happen or be ready soon.

For example: ''The company has several new products in the pipeline for next year.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00397', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 38, 'What does the idiom ''get cold feet'' mean?', 'It means to **become nervous or hesitant about following through on a plan**, especially one previously agreed upon.

For example: ''He got cold feet just before signing the business contract.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00398', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 39, 'What does the idiom ''burn the midnight oil'' mean?', 'It means to **work late into the night**, often to finish an urgent or demanding task.

For example: ''The analysts burned the midnight oil to complete the report before the morning deadline.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00399', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 40, 'What is the difference between ''Affect'' and ''Effect''?', '**''Affect''** is most commonly used as a **verb**, meaning ''to influence'' something — for example, ''The new policy will affect sales.''

**''Effect''** is most commonly used as a **noun**, meaning ''a result or consequence'' — for example, ''The policy had a positive effect on sales.'' (Note: ''effect'' can occasionally be used as a verb meaning ''to bring about,'' as in ''to effect change.'')', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00400', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 41, 'What is the difference between ''Its'' and ''It''s''?', '**''Its''** is a **possessive pronoun**, showing ownership — for example, ''The company increased its revenue.''

**''It''s''** is a **contraction** of ''it is'' or ''it has'' — for example, ''It''s been a difficult quarter.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00401', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 42, 'What is the difference between ''Than'' and ''Then''?', '**''Than''** is used for **comparisons** — for example, ''Revenue was higher than expected.''

**''Then''** relates to **time or sequence** — for example, ''First, we analysed the data; then we presented our findings.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00402', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 43, 'What is the difference between ''Complement'' and ''Compliment''?', '**''Complement''** means something that **completes or goes well with** something else — for example, ''The new software complements the existing system.''

**''Compliment''** means an expression of **praise or admiration** — for example, ''She paid him a compliment on his presentation.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00403', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 44, 'What is the difference between ''Stationary'' and ''Stationery''?', '**''Stationary''** (with an ''a'') is an adjective meaning **''not moving''** — for example, ''The car remained stationary at the signal.''

**''Stationery''** (with an ''e'') is a noun referring to **writing materials**, such as paper, pens, and envelopes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00404', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 45, 'What is the difference between ''Principal'' and ''Principle''?', '**''Principal''** can mean the **head of an institution** (such as a school) or describe something of **primary importance**, as in ''the principal reason'' — it can also refer to the original sum of money in a loan.

**''Principle''** refers to a **fundamental rule, belief, or truth** — for example, ''He acted on the principle of honesty.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00405', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 46, 'What is the difference between ''Elicit'' and ''Illicit''?', '**''Elicit''** is a verb meaning **''to draw out''** a response or reaction — for example, ''The announcement elicited a strong reaction from investors.''

**''Illicit''** is an adjective meaning **''illegal or forbidden''** — for example, ''The company was accused of illicit trading practices.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00406', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 47, 'What is the difference between ''Economic'' and ''Economical''?', '**''Economic''** relates broadly to **the economy or economics** as a field — for example, ''economic policy'' or ''economic growth.''

**''Economical''** means **cost-efficient or thrifty**, describing something that saves money or resources — for example, ''an economical use of fuel.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00407', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 48, 'Change the following sentence to passive voice: ''The manager approved the proposal.''', 'To form the passive voice, the object of the active sentence (''the proposal'') becomes the subject, and the verb changes to a form of ''be'' plus the past participle.

The passive version is: **''The proposal was approved by the manager.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00408', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 49, 'Convert the following sentence to indirect (reported) speech: She said, ''I will finish the report tomorrow.''', 'In reported speech, the tense typically shifts back (will → would), the pronoun changes to match the reporting perspective, and time references like ''tomorrow'' shift accordingly (to ''the next day'').

The indirect version is: **''She said that she would finish the report the next day.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00409', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 50, 'Fill in the blank with the correct preposition: ''The company''s profits are largely dependent _____ consumer demand.''', 'The correct preposition is **''on''**.

''Dependent on'' is the standard idiomatic pairing in English, used whenever one thing relies on or is determined by another.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00410', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 51, 'Fill in the blanks with the correct articles: ''_____ CEO of the company announced _____ 10% increase in salaries.''', 'The first blank should be **''The''**, since it refers to a specific, identifiable person (the one CEO of this particular company).

The second blank should be **''a''**, since ''10%'' begins with a consonant sound (''ten percent''), and it refers to a non-specific, single instance of an increase.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00411', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 52, 'Identify the grammatical error in the sentence: ''Each of the employees have submitted their timesheet.''', 'The error lies in the verb **''have''**.

Since **''each''** is a singular indefinite pronoun, it requires a **singular verb**: the sentence should read, ''Each of the employees **has** submitted their timesheet.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00412', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 53, 'Identify and correct the dangling modifier in the sentence: ''Walking into the office, the computer was already on.''', 'The modifying phrase ''Walking into the office'' is left **''dangling''** because the sentence''s subject, ''the computer,'' cannot logically perform the action of walking.

The corrected sentence should supply a human subject for the phrase to modify: **''Walking into the office, she found the computer already on.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00413', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 54, 'Choose the correct verb form: ''By the time the meeting starts, the presentation _____ (finish).''', 'Since one future action (''the presentation finishing'') will be completed **before another future point in time** (''the meeting starts''), the correct tense is the **future perfect**.

The correct form is: **''By the time the meeting starts, the presentation will have finished.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00414', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 55, 'Correct the lack of parallelism in the sentence: ''The manager''s responsibilities include hiring staff, to train them, and evaluating performance.''', 'The sentence lacks **parallel structure**, since ''hiring'' and ''evaluating'' are gerunds, while ''to train'' is an infinitive — all three items in the list should take the **same grammatical form**.

The corrected sentence is: **''The manager''s responsibilities include hiring staff, training them, and evaluating performance.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00415', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 56, 'Choose the correct verb form for the subjunctive mood: ''If I _____ (be) you, I would accept the offer.''', 'This sentence expresses a **hypothetical, contrary-to-fact condition**, which requires the subjunctive mood. In the subjunctive, the verb ''be'' takes the form **''were''** for all subjects, including ''I''.

The correct sentence is: **''If I were you, I would accept the offer.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00416', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 57, 'Convert the following direct question to indirect (reported) speech: He asked, ''Did you complete the audit?''', 'In reported speech, a yes/no question is introduced with **''if'' or ''whether''**, the question word order reverts to statement order, and the tense shifts back (did → had).

The indirect version is: **''He asked if/whether she had completed the audit.''**', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00417', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 58, 'Which of the following is the correctly spelled word: ''Accomodate'' or ''Accommodate''?', 'The correct spelling is **''Accommodate''**.

A common spelling mistake is to use only one ''c'' or one ''m'' — the correct word has **double ''c'' and double ''m''** (ac-com-mo-date).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00418', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 59, 'Which of the following is the correctly spelled word: ''Occured'' or ''Occurred''?', 'The correct spelling is **''Occurred''**.

When adding ''-ed'' to ''occur'' (which ends in a stressed syllable with a single consonant after a single vowel), the final consonant ''r'' is **doubled**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00419', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 60, 'Which of the following is the correctly spelled word: ''Entreprenuer'' or ''Entrepreneur''?', 'The correct spelling is **''Entrepreneur''**.

A common error is misplacing the ''e'' and ''u'' near the end of the word — remembering it comes from French helps retain the correct **''-eneur''** ending.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00420', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 61, 'Which of the following is the correctly spelled word: ''Liaison'' or ''Liason''?', 'The correct spelling is **''Liaison''**.

A frequent mistake is dropping the first ''i'' — the correct word retains **both ''i''s**, as in ''li-ai-son''.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00421', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 62, 'Which of the following is the correctly spelled word: ''Priviledge'' or ''Privilege''?', 'The correct spelling is **''Privilege''**.

A common error is inserting an extra ''d'' before the ''ge'' — the correct word has **no ''d''**, spelled ''privi-lege''.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00422', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 63, 'Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: ''The company''s growth was remarkably swift.'' (underlined: ''remarkably'')', '''Remarkably'' functions as an **adverb** in this sentence.

It modifies the **adjective ''swift''**, describing the degree or manner of the swiftness — adverbs ending in ''-ly'' commonly modify adjectives, verbs, or other adverbs.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00423', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 64, 'Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: ''The board will review the proposal.'' (underlined: ''review'')', '''Review'' functions as a **verb** in this sentence.

It describes the **action** the board will perform (to examine or assess the proposal), rather than being used as a noun (as in ''a critical review'').', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00424', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 65, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the sentence: ''Time is money.''', 'This is an example of a **metaphor**.

A metaphor makes a **direct comparison** between two unlike things (here, ''time'' and ''money'') without using words like ''like'' or ''as'', implying that time is as valuable and limited as money.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00425', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 66, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the sentence: ''Her words cut like a knife.''', 'This is an example of a **simile**.

A simile makes a comparison between two unlike things using the words **''like'' or ''as''** — here, the sharpness and pain of her words is compared explicitly to the cutting action of a knife.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00426', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 67, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the sentence: ''The headquarters witnessed a flood of complaints.''', 'This is an example of a **metaphor**.

The word **''flood''** is used figuratively (not literally referring to water) to represent an **overwhelming quantity** of complaints arriving at once.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00427', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 68, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the phrase: ''Deafening silence.''', 'This is an example of an **oxymoron**.

An oxymoron combines two seemingly **contradictory terms** (''deafening'', which implies loud noise, and ''silence'', which implies the absence of sound) to create a striking, paradoxical effect emphasising just how complete the silence is.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00428', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 69, 'Identify the figure of speech used in the phrase: ''Bigger, bolder, better.''', 'This is an example of **alliteration**.

Alliteration is the repetition of the **same initial consonant sound** across successive words in a phrase — here, the repeated ''b'' sound creates a catchy, rhythmic emphasis, common in advertising and slogans.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00429', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Vocabulary & Grammar', 5, 70, 'Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: ''Despite the setback, the team persevered.'' (underlined: ''Despite'')', '''Despite'' functions as a **preposition** in this sentence.

It introduces the noun phrase ''the setback'', showing a **contrast** between the setback and the team''s ultimate perseverance — prepositions like ''despite'' and ''in spite of'' are commonly used to express concession.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00430', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 0, 'What is SWOT Analysis, and what does each letter represent?', '**SWOT Analysis** is a strategic planning framework used to evaluate an organisation''s internal and external position.

It stands for **Strengths** and **Weaknesses** (internal factors within the organisation''s control) and **Opportunities** and **Threats** (external factors in the broader environment) — together, this framework helps a business identify where it stands and what strategic actions to pursue.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00431', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 1, 'What are Porter''s Five Forces, and what is the purpose of this framework?', '**Porter''s Five Forces**, developed by Michael Porter, is a framework for analysing the **competitive intensity and attractiveness of an industry**.

The five forces are: **threat of new entrants**, **bargaining power of suppliers**, **bargaining power of buyers**, **threat of substitute products**, and **rivalry among existing competitors** — together, these determine how much profit potential an industry holds for its players.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00432', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 2, 'What is the BCG Matrix, and what are its four quadrants?', 'The **BCG Matrix** (Boston Consulting Group Matrix) is a strategic tool used to **classify a company''s business units or products** based on market growth rate and relative market share.

Its four quadrants are: **Stars** (high growth, high share), **Cash Cows** (low growth, high share), **Question Marks** (high growth, low share), and **Dogs** (low growth, low share) — this classification helps guide resource allocation decisions across a company''s portfolio.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00433', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 3, 'What is Value Chain Analysis, and what are its two broad categories of activities?', '**Value Chain Analysis**, developed by Michael Porter, examines the sequence of activities a company performs to **create and deliver value** to its customers, with the goal of identifying sources of competitive advantage.

Its two broad categories are **Primary Activities** (such as inbound logistics, operations, and marketing — directly involved in creating and delivering the product) and **Support Activities** (such as procurement, technology development, and HR management — which enable the primary activities).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00434', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 4, 'What is PESTEL Analysis, and what does it help assess?', '**PESTEL Analysis** is a framework used to assess the broader **macro-environmental factors** that could impact an organisation''s strategy.

It stands for **Political, Economic, Social, Technological, Environmental, and Legal** factors — unlike SWOT, which looks at a specific organisation, PESTEL focuses on external forces shaping the entire industry or market.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00435', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 5, 'What is ''Blue Ocean Strategy'', and how does it differ from a ''Red Ocean'' approach?', '**Blue Ocean Strategy** refers to a business approach focused on creating **new, uncontested market space**, making existing competition irrelevant by offering something fundamentally different.

A **''Red Ocean''**, by contrast, refers to **existing, saturated markets** where companies compete fiercely over a fixed pool of demand, often leading to price wars and shrinking profit margins — the metaphor contrasts calm ''blue'' uncontested waters with bloody ''red'' competitive ones.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00436', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 6, 'What is meant by ''Core Competency'' in business strategy?', '**Core Competency**, a concept popularised by C.K. Prahalad and Gary Hamel, refers to a **unique combination of skills, knowledge, and capabilities** that gives a company a distinctive advantage and is difficult for competitors to replicate.

A true core competency should provide access to a wide variety of markets, contribute significantly to the perceived customer benefits of the end product, and be hard to imitate.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00437', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 7, 'What is the Ansoff Matrix, and what four growth strategies does it present?', 'The **Ansoff Matrix** is a strategic planning tool that maps **growth strategies** along two dimensions: existing versus new products, and existing versus new markets.

Its four strategies are: **Market Penetration** (existing product, existing market), **Market Development** (existing product, new market), **Product Development** (new product, existing market), and **Diversification** (new product, new market) — the last being generally the riskiest option.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00438', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 8, 'What is ''Benchmarking'' in a business context?', '**Benchmarking** is the practice of comparing a company''s **processes, performance metrics, or practices** against those of industry leaders or best-in-class competitors.

Its purpose is to identify performance gaps and adopt proven best practices, helping a company improve efficiency, quality, or competitiveness relative to external standards.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00439', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 9, 'What are Porter''s two generic competitive strategies for achieving competitive advantage?', 'Michael Porter identified two primary generic strategies: **Cost Leadership** and **Differentiation**.

**Cost Leadership** involves becoming the **lowest-cost producer** in an industry, competing primarily on price, while **Differentiation** involves offering **unique products or features** that customers value enough to pay a premium for, competing on distinctiveness rather than price alone.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00440', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 10, 'What are the 4Ps of the marketing mix?', 'The **4Ps of marketing** are **Product, Price, Place, and Promotion**.

**Product** refers to what is being sold; **Price** refers to how it is valued and charged for; **Place** refers to the distribution channels through which it reaches customers; and **Promotion** refers to the communication and advertising used to market it — together, these form the core controllable elements of a marketing strategy.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00441', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 11, 'What does STP stand for in marketing strategy?', '**STP** stands for **Segmentation, Targeting, and Positioning**.

**Segmentation** divides a broad market into distinct groups of consumers with similar needs; **Targeting** selects which segment(s) to focus on; and **Positioning** defines how the product should be perceived in the minds of customers within that chosen segment, relative to competitors.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00442', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 12, 'What are the four stages of the Product Life Cycle?', 'The **Product Life Cycle** describes the stages a product goes through from launch to decline: **Introduction, Growth, Maturity, and Decline**.

In the **Introduction** stage, sales are low and marketing costs are high; in **Growth**, sales rise rapidly; in **Maturity**, sales peak and stabilise amid intense competition; and in **Decline**, sales fall as the product becomes outdated or demand shifts elsewhere.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00443', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 13, 'What is ''Brand Equity''?', '**Brand Equity** refers to the **commercial value derived from consumer perception** of a brand name, rather than from the product or service itself.

A strong brand with positive brand equity can command **premium pricing, greater customer loyalty, and easier market expansion**, since consumers associate the name itself with quality, trust, or status.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00444', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 14, 'What is a Unique Selling Proposition (USP)?', 'A **Unique Selling Proposition (USP)** is the specific factor or characteristic that **distinguishes a product or service from its competitors**, giving customers a clear reason to choose it over alternatives.

An effective USP is typically something competitors cannot easily replicate, and is often the central theme around which a company''s marketing and advertising are built.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00445', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 15, 'What is ''Marketing Myopia'', a concept introduced by Theodore Levitt?', '**Marketing Myopia** refers to the short-sighted tendency of businesses to **define their industry too narrowly in terms of products, rather than the broader customer need being served**, leading them to miss market shifts and lose relevance.

Levitt''s classic example was railroad companies that saw themselves as being in the ''railroad business'' rather than the ''transportation business'', causing them to fail to adapt as other transportation modes emerged.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00446', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 16, 'What is the difference between Penetration Pricing and Skimming Pricing?', '**Penetration Pricing** involves setting an **initially low price** for a new product to quickly attract customers and gain market share, with prices potentially rising later.

**Skimming Pricing** involves setting an **initially high price** for a new product to capture maximum revenue from early adopters willing to pay a premium, before gradually lowering the price to attract more price-sensitive segments.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00447', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 17, 'What is ''Opportunity Cost'' in economics?', '**Opportunity Cost** refers to the **value of the next best alternative that is foregone** when a choice is made between competing options.

For example, if a company invests its capital in a new factory rather than in financial markets, the opportunity cost is the **return it could have earned** had it chosen the alternative investment instead.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00448', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 18, 'What is Price Elasticity of Demand, and how is it calculated?', '**Price Elasticity of Demand** measures how sensitive the **quantity demanded** of a good is to a change in its price.

It is calculated as `% change in quantity demanded / % change in price`; demand is considered **''elastic''** if this value exceeds 1 (quantity is highly sensitive to price) and **''inelastic''** if it is less than 1 (quantity changes little despite price changes).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00449', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 19, 'What are ''Economies of Scale''?', '**Economies of Scale** refer to the **cost advantages** a business gains as its **output or production volume increases**, typically because fixed costs get spread over a larger number of units.

This can arise from factors such as bulk purchasing discounts, more efficient use of specialised machinery, and spreading overhead costs — resulting in a **lower average cost per unit** as production scales up.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00450', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 20, 'What is the Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns?', 'The **Law of Diminishing Marginal Returns** states that, when one input (such as labour) is increased while other inputs (such as capital or land) remain fixed, the **additional output gained from each extra unit of that input will eventually decline**.

For example, adding more workers to a factory with a fixed number of machines will initially boost output, but beyond a certain point, each additional worker contributes progressively less, due to overcrowding or diminishing access to the fixed resources.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00451', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 21, 'What is the difference between Demand-Pull Inflation and Cost-Push Inflation?', '**Demand-Pull Inflation** occurs when **aggregate demand in an economy exceeds aggregate supply**, pulling prices upward as too much money chases too few goods.

**Cost-Push Inflation** occurs when the **cost of production inputs rises** (such as wages or raw material prices), forcing producers to raise prices to maintain their margins, even without a corresponding rise in demand.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00452', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 22, 'What are the key differences between Perfect Competition and Monopoly market structures?', '**Perfect Competition** is characterised by **many buyers and sellers**, identical (homogeneous) products, free entry and exit, and no single firm having any influence over market price — firms are ''price takers.''

A **Monopoly**, by contrast, has a **single seller** with no close substitutes for its product, significant barriers to entry for competitors, and the ability to influence or set the market price — the firm is a ''price maker.''', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00453', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 23, 'What is an ''Externality'' in economics, and what are its two types?', 'An **Externality** is a cost or benefit arising from an economic activity that **affects a third party who did not choose to be involved** in that activity.

A **Positive Externality** provides unintended benefits to others (such as a vaccinated individual reducing disease spread to others), while a **Negative Externality** imposes unintended costs on others (such as factory pollution harming nearby residents'' health).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00454', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 24, 'What is a ''Public Good'' in economics, and what two properties define it?', 'A **Public Good** is a good or service defined by two key properties: it is **non-excludable** (no one can be effectively prevented from using it) and **non-rivalrous** (one person''s use does not reduce its availability to others).

Classic examples include national defence and street lighting — because private firms cannot easily charge users individually for such goods, they are typically provided by governments.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00455', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 25, 'What is the Law of Demand, and what is the Law of Supply?', 'The **Law of Demand** states that, all else being equal, as the **price** of a good **rises, the quantity demanded falls**, and vice versa — reflecting an inverse relationship between price and demand.

The **Law of Supply** states that, all else being equal, as the **price** of a good **rises, the quantity supplied also rises**, reflecting a direct relationship between price and the willingness of producers to supply more.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00456', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 26, 'What is the Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility?', 'The **Law of Diminishing Marginal Utility** states that as a person **consumes successive additional units** of a good, the **additional satisfaction (utility)** gained from each extra unit **tends to decrease**.

For instance, the satisfaction from eating the first slice of pizza is typically greater than the satisfaction from eating the fifth slice — this principle helps explain why demand curves generally slope downward.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00457', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 27, 'What is ''Monopolistic Competition'', and how does it differ from a Monopoly?', '**Monopolistic Competition** is a market structure featuring **many sellers offering differentiated (but substitutable) products**, such as branded clothing or restaurants, each with some control over pricing due to brand loyalty or perceived uniqueness.

This differs from a true **Monopoly**, where there is only **one seller** with no close substitutes at all — in monopolistic competition, competitors exist and can act as substitutes, just not perfect ones.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00458', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 28, 'What is ''Elasticity of Supply''?', '**Elasticity of Supply** measures how responsive the **quantity supplied** of a good is to a change in its price.

It is calculated as `% change in quantity supplied / % change in price`; supply is considered **''elastic''** if producers can quickly ramp up output in response to a price rise (common for goods with flexible production), and **''inelastic''** if output cannot be quickly adjusted (common for goods with long production lead times, like agricultural crops).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00459', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 29, 'What is ''Working Capital'', and how is it calculated?', '**Working Capital** represents the funds a company has available for its **day-to-day operations**, reflecting its short-term financial health and liquidity.

It is calculated as `Working Capital = Current Assets − Current Liabilities`; positive working capital indicates a company can comfortably meet its short-term obligations, while negative working capital can signal potential liquidity trouble.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00460', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 30, 'What is the key difference between a Balance Sheet and a Profit & Loss (Income) Statement?', 'A **Balance Sheet** is a **snapshot at a specific point in time**, showing a company''s assets, liabilities, and shareholders'' equity — essentially, what the company owns and owes.

A **Profit & Loss Statement** (or Income Statement) covers a **period of time** (such as a quarter or year), showing the company''s revenues, expenses, and resulting net profit or loss over that period.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00461', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 31, 'What is the difference between the Straight-Line and Written-Down Value (Declining Balance) methods of depreciation?', 'The **Straight-Line Method** depreciates an asset by an **equal fixed amount every year** over its useful life, calculated as `(Cost − Salvage Value) / Useful Life`.

The **Written-Down Value (Declining Balance) Method** applies a **fixed percentage to the asset''s remaining book value** each year, resulting in **larger depreciation charges in earlier years** and progressively smaller charges as the asset ages.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00462', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 32, 'What is Break-even Analysis, and how is the break-even point calculated?', '**Break-even Analysis** determines the **level of sales at which total revenue equals total costs**, meaning the business makes neither a profit nor a loss.

The break-even point (in units) is calculated as `Fixed Costs / (Selling Price per Unit − Variable Cost per Unit)` — the denominator here is known as the **''contribution margin''** per unit.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00463', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 33, 'What is the difference between Financial Leverage and Operating Leverage?', '**Financial Leverage** refers to the extent to which a company uses **debt (borrowed funds)** to finance its assets and operations — higher financial leverage magnifies both potential returns and potential losses to shareholders.

**Operating Leverage** refers to the extent to which a company''s cost structure relies on **fixed costs versus variable costs** — a company with high operating leverage sees its profits fluctuate more sharply with changes in sales volume, since fixed costs don''t change with output.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00464', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 34, 'What is the ''Time Value of Money'', and why is it a foundational concept in finance?', 'The **Time Value of Money** is the principle that a **given sum of money is worth more today than the same sum in the future**, because money available now can be invested to earn returns over time.

This concept underlies virtually all financial decision-making, including loan interest calculations, investment appraisal, and valuation — it''s why financial techniques like NPV and IRR explicitly ''discount'' future cash flows back to their present-day value.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00465', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 35, 'What is Net Present Value (NPV), and how is it used in investment decisions?', '**Net Present Value (NPV)** is the **sum of the present values of all expected future cash flows** from an investment, minus the initial investment cost — it accounts for the time value of money by discounting future cash flows back to today''s value.

A project is generally considered **financially worthwhile if its NPV is positive**, meaning the discounted returns exceed the initial outlay; a negative NPV suggests the investment would destroy value.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00466', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 36, 'What is the Internal Rate of Return (IRR)?', 'The **Internal Rate of Return (IRR)** is the **discount rate at which a project''s Net Present Value (NPV) equals exactly zero** — in other words, the rate of return the project is expected to generate on its own.

A project is typically considered acceptable if its **IRR exceeds the company''s required rate of return (cost of capital)**; companies often use IRR alongside NPV to compare and rank competing investment opportunities.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00467', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 37, 'What is the Current Ratio, and what does it indicate about a company?', 'The **Current Ratio** is a liquidity metric calculated as `Current Assets / Current Liabilities`.

It indicates a company''s ability to **meet its short-term obligations** using its short-term assets; a ratio above 1 generally suggests the company has sufficient current assets to cover its current liabilities, though an excessively high ratio might also suggest inefficient use of assets.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00468', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 38, 'What is the Quick Ratio (Acid-Test Ratio), and how does it differ from the Current Ratio?', 'The **Quick Ratio**, also called the **Acid-Test Ratio**, is calculated as `(Current Assets − Inventory) / Current Liabilities`.

It differs from the Current Ratio by **excluding inventory**, since inventory can take longer to convert into cash — the Quick Ratio therefore gives a stricter, more conservative measure of a company''s immediate short-term liquidity.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00469', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 39, 'What is a Cash Flow Statement, and what are its three main categories?', 'A **Cash Flow Statement** tracks the actual **inflows and outflows of cash** within a business over a given period, distinct from the accrual-based figures shown in the Income Statement.

Its three main categories are: **Operating Activities** (cash from core business operations), **Investing Activities** (cash used for or generated by buying/selling long-term assets), and **Financing Activities** (cash from borrowing, repaying debt, or transactions with shareholders).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00470', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 40, 'What is ''Goodwill'' in accounting?', '**Goodwill** is an **intangible asset** that arises when a company acquires another business for a price **higher than the fair value of its identifiable net assets**.

It represents the value of non-physical factors such as **brand reputation, customer relationships, and employee expertise** that the acquirer is willing to pay for, beyond the target''s tangible book value.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00471', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 41, 'What are the five levels of Maslow''s Hierarchy of Needs?', '**Maslow''s Hierarchy of Needs** is a motivational theory arranging human needs in a pyramid, from most basic to most advanced: **Physiological needs** (food, water, shelter), **Safety needs** (security, stability), **Social/Love and Belonging needs** (relationships, friendship), **Esteem needs** (recognition, respect), and **Self-Actualisation** (realising one''s full potential).

The theory suggests that **lower-level needs must generally be satisfied before higher-level needs** become significant motivators of behaviour.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00472', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 42, 'What is Herzberg''s Two-Factor Theory of motivation?', '**Herzberg''s Two-Factor Theory** distinguishes between **''Hygiene Factors''** and **''Motivators''** in workplace satisfaction.

**Hygiene Factors** (such as salary, company policy, and working conditions) do not actively motivate employees when present, but their **absence causes dissatisfaction**; **Motivators** (such as recognition, achievement, and growth opportunities) are what actually **drive higher satisfaction and performance** when present.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00473', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 43, 'What is meant by ''Span of Control'' in organisational management?', '**Span of Control** refers to the **number of subordinates that a manager or supervisor directly oversees**.

A **''narrow'' span of control** (fewer subordinates per manager) typically allows closer supervision but results in more hierarchical levels, while a **''wide'' span of control** (more subordinates per manager) results in a flatter organisational structure but less direct oversight of each individual.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00474', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 44, 'What is a ''Matrix Organisational Structure''?', 'A **Matrix Organisational Structure** is one in which employees report to **two (or more) managers simultaneously** — typically a functional manager (e.g., head of engineering) and a project or product manager.

This structure is designed to combine the benefits of **functional specialisation** with the **flexibility of project-based teams**, though it can create challenges around divided authority and conflicting priorities.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00475', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 45, 'What is Kurt Lewin''s three-step model of Change Management?', '**Kurt Lewin''s model** describes organisational change as occurring in three stages: **''Unfreeze''**, **''Change''**, and **''Refreeze''**.

**''Unfreeze''** involves preparing the organisation to accept that change is needed; **''Change''** is the actual transition period where new processes or behaviours are implemented; and **''Refreeze''** involves stabilising the organisation in its new state so the change becomes the new normal.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00476', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 46, 'What is the difference between Employee Attrition and Employee Retention?', '**Employee Attrition** refers to the **gradual reduction of staff** as employees leave the organisation (voluntarily or involuntarily) and are not replaced, often measured as a rate over a given period.

**Employee Retention** refers to an organisation''s **ability to keep its employees** and reduce voluntary turnover, typically through strategies like competitive compensation, career growth opportunities, and positive workplace culture.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00477', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 47, 'What are ''Economies of Scope'', and how do they differ from Economies of Scale?', '**Economies of Scope** refer to the **cost savings a company achieves by producing a variety of related products together**, rather than producing each one separately — for example, a dairy company producing both milk and cheese using shared raw materials and distribution.

This differs from **Economies of Scale**, which refers to cost savings from producing a **larger volume of a single product**; scope economies come from **diversity and shared resources**, while scale economies come from **sheer production volume**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00478', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 48, 'What is ''First-Mover Advantage''?', '**First-Mover Advantage** refers to the **competitive benefits a company gains by being the first to enter a new market or introduce a new product category**.

These benefits can include **building strong brand recognition, securing scarce resources or distribution channels, and establishing customer loyalty** before competitors arrive — though being first does not guarantee long-term success, as later entrants can sometimes learn from and improve upon the pioneer''s mistakes.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00479', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 49, 'What is ''Disruptive Innovation'', a concept introduced by Clayton Christensen?', '**Disruptive Innovation** describes a process by which a **smaller company with fewer resources successfully challenges established, larger incumbent businesses**, typically by initially targeting overlooked market segments with a simpler or cheaper offering.

Over time, the disruptive product **improves in quality and moves upmarket**, eventually displacing the established competitors who were focused on serving their most demanding, high-margin customers and failed to respond to the lower-end threat.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00480', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 50, 'What does it mean for a startup to ''Pivot''?', 'A **''Pivot''** refers to a startup making a **fundamental change in its business strategy**, such as its product, target market, or revenue model, typically in response to evidence that the original approach isn''t working.

Rather than shutting down entirely, a pivot allows a company to **retain its existing team, resources, and some validated learnings** while redirecting toward a strategy more likely to succeed.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00481', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 51, 'What does it mean for a startup to be ''Bootstrapped''?', 'A **''Bootstrapped''** startup is one that is **built and grown using the founders'' own personal savings and the company''s own operating revenues**, rather than relying on external funding such as venture capital or angel investment.

Bootstrapping allows founders to **retain full ownership and control** of their company, though it can also limit the speed at which the business can scale compared to well-funded competitors.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00482', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 52, 'What is ''Burn Rate'' in the context of a startup?', '**Burn Rate** refers to the **rate at which a company (typically a startup not yet profitable) spends its available cash reserves** over a given period, usually expressed monthly.

A high burn rate relative to a company''s remaining cash (its ''runway'') signals that the company will need to **raise additional funding soon or achieve profitability quickly**, or risk running out of money.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00483', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 53, 'What is the concept of ''Comparative Advantage'' in international trade?', '**Comparative Advantage**, a concept developed by economist David Ricardo, states that a country should **specialise in producing the goods it can produce at the lowest opportunity cost** relative to other countries, and trade for the rest.

Crucially, a country can benefit from trade through comparative advantage even if another country is **more efficient at producing everything** (has an absolute advantage in all goods), as long as relative opportunity costs differ between them.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00484', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 54, 'What is ''Absolute Advantage'', and how does it differ from Comparative Advantage?', '**Absolute Advantage**, a concept from Adam Smith, refers to a country''s ability to **produce a good using fewer resources (or more efficiently) than another country**.

This differs from **Comparative Advantage**, which focuses on **relative opportunity costs** rather than absolute efficiency — a country can lack an absolute advantage in producing anything, yet still benefit from trade by specialising according to its comparative advantage.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00485', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 55, 'What is the ''Balance of Trade''?', 'The **Balance of Trade** is the difference between the value of a country''s **exports and imports of goods** over a given period.

A **trade surplus** occurs when exports exceed imports, while a **trade deficit** occurs when imports exceed exports — it is a key component (though not the entirety) of a country''s broader current account in its Balance of Payments.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00486', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 56, 'What is the difference between Protectionism and Free Trade?', '**Protectionism** refers to government policies that **restrict international trade** to shield domestic industries from foreign competition, using tools like tariffs, quotas, and subsidies.

**Free Trade** refers to the **absence of such government-imposed barriers**, allowing goods and services to flow between countries based purely on market forces and comparative advantage — the two represent opposite ends of a trade-policy spectrum.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00487', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 57, 'What is the difference between a Fixed and a Floating Exchange Rate system?', 'Under a **Fixed Exchange Rate** system, a country''s central bank **pegs its currency''s value to another currency** (or a basket of currencies) and actively intervenes in currency markets to maintain that peg.

Under a **Floating Exchange Rate** system, a currency''s value is **determined by market forces of supply and demand**, fluctuating freely based on trade flows, interest rates, and investor sentiment, without direct central bank intervention to maintain a specific rate.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00488', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 58, 'What is ''Dumping'' in the context of international trade?', '**Dumping** refers to the practice of a company **exporting a product to another country at a price lower than it charges in its home market** (or below its cost of production), often to gain market share rapidly or eliminate competition.

Countries facing dumping can impose **anti-dumping duties** on the affected imports, as permitted under WTO rules, to protect their domestic industries from this unfair pricing practice.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00489', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 59, 'What does ''Limited Liability'' mean for a company''s shareholders?', '**Limited Liability** means that the **personal financial liability of a company''s shareholders is limited to the amount they invested** in the company''s shares.

This means that if the company fails or incurs debts it cannot repay, shareholders'' **personal assets** (beyond their original investment) **cannot be seized** by creditors to satisfy the company''s obligations — this protection is a key reason businesses choose to incorporate.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00490', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 60, 'What are the key differences between a Sole Proprietorship, a Partnership, and a Corporation?', 'A **Sole Proprietorship** is owned and run by a single individual, who bears **unlimited personal liability** for all business debts.

A **Partnership** involves two or more owners sharing profits, responsibilities, and (typically) **unlimited liability** between them, unless structured as a Limited Liability Partnership.

A **Corporation** is a separate legal entity from its owners, offering shareholders **limited liability**, but subject to more extensive regulatory and compliance requirements than the other two forms.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00491', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 61, 'What is ''Corporate Governance''?', '**Corporate Governance** refers to the **system of rules, practices, and processes** by which a company is directed and controlled, balancing the interests of its many stakeholders — shareholders, management, employees, customers, and the broader community.

Key elements include the role and composition of the **board of directors**, transparency in financial reporting, executive accountability, and protection of **shareholder rights**.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00492', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 62, 'What is ''Fiduciary Duty'' in a corporate context?', '**Fiduciary Duty** refers to the **legal obligation of a person in a position of trust** (such as a company director or officer) to act in the **best interests of another party** (such as the company''s shareholders), rather than their own personal interests.

It typically encompasses both a **''duty of care''** (acting with reasonable competence and diligence) and a **''duty of loyalty''** (avoiding conflicts of interest and self-dealing).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00493', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 63, 'What are the three main types of Intellectual Property Rights, and what does each protect?', 'The three main types are **Patents**, **Trademarks**, and **Copyrights**.

A **Patent** protects a **new invention or process**, granting the inventor exclusive rights for a limited period; a **Trademark** protects **brand identifiers** such as names, logos, and symbols used to distinguish goods or services; and a **Copyright** protects **original creative or literary works**, such as books, music, and films.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00494', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 64, 'What is a Limited Liability Partnership (LLP), and how does it differ from a traditional partnership?', 'A **Limited Liability Partnership (LLP)** is a hybrid business structure that combines the **operational flexibility of a traditional partnership** with the **limited liability protection** typically associated with a corporation.

In a traditional partnership, partners usually bear **unlimited personal liability** for business debts and for the actions of other partners; in an LLP, each partner''s liability is generally **limited to their own actions and investment**, protecting them from the misconduct or negligence of other partners.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00495', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 65, 'What is the key difference between Monetary Policy and Fiscal Policy?', '**Monetary Policy** is implemented by a country''s **central bank** (such as the RBI), using tools like interest rates and reserve requirements to influence the **money supply, credit availability, and inflation**.

**Fiscal Policy** is implemented by the **government**, using tools like **taxation and public spending** to influence overall economic activity, employment, and growth — the two operate through different institutions and different levers, though they are often coordinated.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00496', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 66, 'What are the four phases of the Business Cycle?', 'The **Business Cycle** describes the natural fluctuation of economic activity over time, typically divided into four phases: **Expansion** (growing output and employment), **Peak** (the highest point of the cycle, before growth begins to slow), **Contraction/Recession** (declining output, rising unemployment), and **Trough** (the lowest point, before recovery and expansion begin again).', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00497', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 67, 'What is the ''Multiplier Effect'' in Keynesian economics?', 'The **Multiplier Effect** describes how an initial **increase in spending** (such as government expenditure) leads to a **larger overall increase in national income**, because that initial spending becomes income for others, who in turn spend a portion of it, and so on through successive rounds.

For example, government spending on infrastructure pays wages to workers, who then spend part of those wages on goods and services, generating income for other businesses and workers — the final increase in total economic output exceeds the original spending injection.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00498', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 68, 'What is the ''Crowding Out Effect'' in macroeconomics?', 'The **Crowding Out Effect** describes a situation where **increased government borrowing** (to finance a fiscal deficit) leads to **higher interest rates**, which in turn **reduces or ''crowds out'' private sector investment and spending**.

This occurs because government borrowing competes with private borrowers for the same pool of available loanable funds, pushing up the cost of borrowing for businesses and individuals alike.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00499', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 69, 'What is ''Stagflation''?', '**Stagflation** is an economic condition characterised by the simultaneous occurrence of **stagnant economic growth, high unemployment, and high inflation** — a combination that classical economic theory traditionally considered unlikely to occur together.

The term and the phenomenon became prominent during the **1970s oil crisis**, when supply-side shocks (sharp oil price increases) caused both rising prices and economic stagnation simultaneously.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');
INSERT INTO questions (id, set_id, topic_label, topic_order, sort_order, q, a, created_at) VALUES ('mba-q00500', 'mba-entrance-bank', 'Business & Economy Concepts', 6, 70, 'What is the key difference between Supply-Side and Demand-Side economics?', '**Supply-Side Economics** emphasises policies that **boost the economy''s productive capacity**, such as tax cuts for businesses and investors, deregulation, and incentives to increase production and investment.

**Demand-Side Economics** (rooted in Keynesian theory) emphasises policies that **boost overall spending and demand** in the economy, such as government spending programmes or tax cuts for consumers, particularly during downturns when private demand is weak.', '2026-11-14T06:17:56.365Z');

